\u2192 4 tiles left.  \n\nOur melds: F1 pung, T1 pung, and new W4-W5-W6 or W5-W6-W7 Chow.  \n\nWe need to consider if this improves winning chances:  \nWe have 2 T7 (potential pung), J2 (potential pair or part of dragon pung), F3 (potential pung of West Wind, which is our seat wind \u2192 2 points), T2 (isolated).  \n\nIf we keep T7 pair and J2 pair, we could aim for:  \n- All Pungs (6 points) \u2014 we already have 2 pungs, need 2 more pungs.  \n- Seat Wind pung (F3 pung) would give 2 points.  \n- Dragon pung (J2 pung) would give 2 points.  \n- All Types (6 points) \u2014 we have W in new Chow, T in T1 pung, B? none yet, F in F1 pung, J in J2 possible pung. Missing B suit for All Types, so maybe not.  \n\nIf we take Chi, we get closer to a hand with multiple pungs, but we also lose flexibility since Chow is exposed.  \n\nAlternative: Pass. If we pass, we keep hand intact and draw a tile next turn, possibly getting closer to a concealed hand (Concealed Hand = 2 points if win by discard, Fully Concealed = 4 points if win by self-draw).  \n\nGiven we already have 2 exposed pungs, Fully Concealed is impossible, but Concealed Hand is still possible if all remaining melds are concealed and we win by discard. Currently we have 2 exposed pungs, so Concealed Hand pattern is gone. So no extra points for concealment.  \n\nAlso, Chi now might give us a faster route to a winning hand if we draw well, but passing keeps more options (like going for Seven Pairs? We have 7 tiles, not enough pairs yet).  \n\n**Unseen tiles info:**  \nW9, T1, F1 are all 0 left \u2014 our F1 and T1 pungs used them up.  \nMany other tiles plentiful.  \n\n**Scoring potential:**  \nCurrent possible patterns:  \n- All Pungs (6) if we get 2 more pungs.  \n- Dragon Pung (2) if we get J2 pung.  \n- Seat Wind Pung (2) if we get F3 pung.  \n- Pung of Terminals or Honors (1 each) \u2014 F1 pung already gives 1, T1 pung gives 1.  \n- No Honors? No, we have honors.  \n- Half Flush? We have W, T, F, J \u2014 not single suit.  \n\nWe need 8+ points to win. Currently from melds: F1 pung = 1 (Pung of Honors), T1 pung = 1 (Pung of Terminals). That\u2019s 2 points. Need 6 more.  \n\nIf we get F3 pung (Seat Wind) = 2, J2 pung (Dragon) = 2, that\u2019s 4 more \u2192 total 6, still short.  \nAll Pungs adds 6 \u2192 total could be 12 if we get 4 pungs total (we already have 2).  \n\nBut with a Chow (if we Chi now), All Pungs is impossible. So Chi wrecks All Pungs chance.  \n\nThus, passing is better because we can still aim for All Pungs (need two more pungs from T7, J2, F3, or new draws).  \n\nAlso, after Chi, we\u2019d have a Chow and 2 Pungs, need one more meld+pair, but All Pungs gone, so harder to reach 8 points unless we get other high-point patterns.  \n\nGiven this, **Pass** seems better to keep All Pungs possibility alive.  \n\n---\n\n**Evaluation:** Passing maintains the chance for All Pungs (6 points) combined with Dragon Pung (2) and Seat Wind Pung (2) and Pung of Terminals/Honors (1 each from existing) to exceed 8 points. Chi W5 would commit to a Chow and lose All Pungs, making it harder to reach 8 points unless we get other high-value patterns, which is less likely.\n\n**Reason:** We already have two pungs (F1, T1), so pursuing two more pungs (from F3, J2, T7, or new draws) for All Pungs is a viable path to 8+ points. Chi W5 would ruin that chance and not significantly improve other high-point patterns.\n\n**Answer:** Pass"}
