6, F2, B7, W2, J1, B5. They discarded F3 once, so F3 might be somewhat safe? But I need F3.  \n\nGiven I want to keep F3 and J2, and T7 pair, I should discard a suited tile that is less likely to be dangerous. B1 was discarded by previous player, so maybe safe. B2 and B3 not discarded much. But also consider tile counts: B1 unshown 3, B2 unshown 3, B3 unshown 4. All have some left.  \n\nAlso, I might consider discarding W6 because it is a single and not contributing to pungs. But W6 could be used in a chow if I change plan. But if I commit to all pungs, discard W6.  \n\nEvaluating a few promising moves:  \n1. Play B1: Breaks the chow, keeps B2,B3 which could potentially form a pair or meld if I draw B2 or B3, but for all pungs, I don't need chows. So discarding B1 is fine.  \n2. Play B3: Similarly.  \n3. Play J2: But I need J2 for dragon pung, so not good.  \n4. Play W6: Removes a single, keeps B123 chow, which might be useful if I fall back to mixed hand.  \n5. Play F3: But F3 is seat wind, valuable for pung, so not good.  \n6. Play B2: Similar to B1.  \n7. Play T7: Would break the pair, which I need for winning hand, so not good.  \n\nBest is to discard a suited tile from the B123 chow. Among B1, B2, B3, which is least useful? Since I have no other B tiles, any is fine. But consider future draws: If I draw another B1, I could have a pair of B1, but I already have T7 pair. So not necessary.  \n\nAlso, think about potential points: If I go for all pungs, I need to get two more F3 and two more J2. That's challenging but possible given tile counts. Alternatively, I could also consider going for \"Half Flush\" if I keep suited tiles of one suit plus honors. But I have mixed suits.  \n\nGiven the exposed melds are F1 and T1, which are honors and terminals, and I have seat wind F3, aiming for seat wind pung and dragon pung seems promising for points.  \n\nI'll choose to discard B1 to break the chow and keep the honors and pair.  \n\nReason: Discarding B1 breaks the potential chow but preserves the T7 pair and the honors F3 (seat wind) and J2 (dragon), which are valuable for scoring patterns like Seat Wind Pung (2 points) and Dragon Pung (2 points). Combined with the existing pungs of F1 and T1, aiming for an All Pungs hand (6 points) could yield at least 12 points, meeting the 8-point threshold. B1 is relatively safe as it has been discarded before, and keeping B2 and B3 maintains flexibility for future draws.  \n\nAnswer: Play B1"}
