he honors? But honors give points.  \n\nLet's evaluate each discard:  \n\n1. Play B1: keeps T6, B3, F3, J2. B1 is dot, can form chow with B2,B3 but B2 only 1 left. B1 pung possible with 2 left. Discarding B1 removes dot terminal? B1 is terminal (1), yes, so discarding terminal might reduce chance for Pung of Terminals or Honors? But I already have T1 pung, so I have that pattern. B1 pung would also give Pung of Terminals or Honors (1 point). But if I discard it, I lose that chance. However, B1 is terminal, and terminals are useful for Outside Hand? Not applicable.  \n\n2. Play T6: keeps B1, B3, F3, J2. T6 is simple bamboo, no extra points. Discarding T6 keeps dots and honors.  \n\n3. Play B3: keeps T6, B1, F3, J2. B3 is simple dot, many left, good for pung. Discarding it might be wasteful.  \n\n4. Play J2: keeps T6, B1, B3, F3. Loses dragon potential.  \n\n5. Play F3: keeps T6, B1, B3, J2. Loses Seat Wind potential.  \n\nI think F3 is Seat Wind, which is 2 points if pung. J2 is dragon, 2 points if pung. Both valuable. B1 is terminal, 1 point if pung. B3 is simple, no extra point. T6 is simple.  \n\nSo least valuable for points is B3 or T6. Between B3 and T6, B3 has more tiles left (4 vs 3), so better chance to pung. So discard T6 might be better than discard B3.  \n\nBut T6 can form chow with T4,T5. T4 and T5 have 2 left each, possible. B3 can form chow with B1,B2 or B2,B4 etc.  \n\nMaybe discard T6 because bamboo is already present in my melds (T1, T7), so keeping bamboo might help Half Flush? Half Flush requires only one suit and honors. If I keep bamboo and discard dots, I could aim for Half Flush. But I have dots in hand, so to achieve Half Flush, I need to get rid of all dots. That means discarding B1 and B3 eventually. So maybe discard B1 or B3 now.  \n\nBut Half Flush is 6 points, plus other patterns could reach 8. Currently I have 2 points from pungs of terminals/honors. If I get Half Flush (6) + Pung of Terminals/Honors (at least 1 more from F3 or J2) = 9 points. That works.  \n\nTo achieve Half Flush, I need all suited tiles to be bamboo. My melds: T1 and T7 are bamboo, good. My hand: currently T6 (bamboo), B1 (dot), B3 (dot), F3 (wind), J2 (dragon). So I need to change B1 and B3 into bamboo tiles. That means I need to draw bamboo tiles and discard B1 and B3. So I should discard B1 or B3 now.  \n\nWhich one? B1 is terminal, B3 is simple. If I discard B1, I lose terminal, but that's okay. B3 has more tiles left, so keeping B3 might be better for temporary flexibility? But for Half Flush, I want to discard both eventually.  \n\nMaybe discard B1 now, keep B3 because B3 can pair/pung more easily, and later if I draw bamboo, discard B3.  \n\nBut also consider that opponents' discards might give clues. History shows many dots and bamboo discarded.  \n\nI think aiming for Half Flush is reasonable. So discard a dot. Between B1 and B3, B1 is terminal, less useful for chows because terminals only chow 1-2-3 or 7-8-9. B1 can only chow with B2,B3. B3 can chow with B1,B2 or B2,B4 or B4,B5. So B3 is more flexible for chows if I temporarily keep it while waiting to convert to bamboo. But since I aim for Half Flush, I don't want dot chows, I want bamboo chows. So flexibility not needed.  \n\nMaybe discard B1 because it's terminal and might be safer discard? Terminals are often discarded early.  \n\nBut looking at unshown tiles: B1 has 2 left, B3 has 4 left. Discarding B1 might be less likely to give opponent a pung? Not sure.  \n\nI think I'll discard B1 to start moving towards Half Flush.  \n\nReason: Discarding B1 (terminal dot) reduces the dot suit in hand, aiming for Half Flush (only bamboo and honors), which combined with existing patterns can yield at least 8 points. Keeping B3 temporarily for potential pair/pung while drawing bamboo tiles.  \n\nFinal choice: Play B1.  \n\nEvaluation: Discarding B1 is a step towards Half Flush, keeps B3 for flexibility, and retains honors for extra points.  \n\nReason: To pursue Half Flush (6 